Dear list,

I vaguely remember a discussion about this but I can't find it in the
archives. Consider the following example,

x <- seq(0, 2, length=1e4)
d <- data.frame(x=x, y=1/((x-1)^2 + 0.01) )
d$y[d$x < 1] <-  d$x[d$x < 1] -1

p <- qplot(x, y, data=d, geom="path")

p + ylim(-10, 100)
p + ylim(-10, 60)

The first graph shows a sharp but (artificially) continuous curve,
while the second graph cuts the path which becomes discontinuous. I
was hoping to have a vertical segment at x=1 that would be clipped at
y=60 instead of this discontinuity in the path. It's what base
graphics does,

plot(d, ylim=c(-10, 60), t="l")

and also lattice,

library(lattice)
xyplot(y~x, d, type="l", ylim=c(-10, 60))

Any tips?

Best regards,

baptiste

